  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "Consider the Diophantine equation $5x+6y=9.$ The integer pair $(\\overset{∼}{x},\\overset{∼}{y})$ is the solution with the smallest possible positive value of $x.$ What is the value of $\\overset{∼}{x}?$",
  "worked_solution": "We have the linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere $a=5,$ $b=6,$ and $c=9.$ We're given that the equation has solutions.\n\nNotice that our equation can't be solved for either of the two variables using integer coefficients. So, let's find the linear representation of $\\text{gcd}(5,6)$ using the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}6 & = & 5\\cdot 1 & + & 1 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 5 & = & 1\\cdot 5 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =6-5\\cdot 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ and\n\n$$\n5\\cdot (-1)+6=1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this equation by $9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot (-1)+6 & =1 \\\\ 5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 9+6\\cdot 9 & =9 \\\\ 5\\cdot (-9)+6\\cdot 9 & =9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $(x,y)=(-9,9)$ is a solution to our Diophantine equation.\n\nNow, the general solution is\n\n$$\n(-9+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,9-\\frac{a}{d}t)=(-9+6t,\\,9-5t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\\,.$\n\nThe smallest possible positive value of $x=-9+6t$ is attained when $t=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{∼}{x}=-9+6\\cdot 2=3.\n$$",
